 # Progressive and Multi-stage Training
 
-Also called: Curriculum Learning for 3DGS, Stage-wise Optimization
+Also called: Progressive Densification, Curriculum Learning for 3DGS, Multi-stage Optimization
 
 ## Overview
-Progressive and Multi-stage Training structures the optimization of 3D Gaussian primitives into discrete phases to establish a valid geometric backbone before refining details [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike single-stage training, which often converges to suboptimal local minima or exhibits poor structural fidelity, this approach decouples initialization from refinement, yielding significantly higher rendering quality [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Curriculum Learning with Slice and Voxel Supervision](12921-curriculum-learning-with-slice-and-voxel-supervision.md) by focusing on the topological evolution of the primitive set (mesh or point cloud) rather than the supervision signal itself.
+Progressive and Multi-stage Training refers to optimization strategies that decompose the 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) training process into sequential phases or dynamic stages, rather than optimizing a single static configuration from initialization [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of training instability, insufficient geometric detail, and error propagation inherent in single-stage end-to-end optimization, where primitives may fail to capture high-frequency details or become redundant in smooth regions [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard densification strategies by explicitly decoupling distinct learning objectives (e.g., geometry vs. pose, local structure vs. global coverage) or by dynamically adjusting primitive density based on error metrics during training [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
